WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:05.020
Hello everyone, I&#x27;m Michael, a senior at Kehillah High School.

2
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.500
This is my personal portfolio and I&#x27;m going to walk through some projects together with you.

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.020
It starts on a lake.

4
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.780
This is the school&#x27;s Sailbot simulator running on a PC with the autopilot steering.

5
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.840
Underneath, the code works out the forces on the boat, moves it a tiny step and repeats.

6
00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.340
Motion is just a state updating on a clock.

7
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.360
Next came a class investigation, shown here in its raw log.

8
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.340
If you write a graph as a grid and multiply it by itself, each number counts the routes between two points.

9
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.060
Sign-Translator&#x27;s skeleton is that kind of graph.

10
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.660
The plain Fourier transform takes almost 9 seconds on 8,000 samples.

11
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.100
When you split the signal in half and recurse, the FFT takes just 26 milliseconds.

12
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:54.760
Later on, a spectrum like this revealed that Sign-Translator&#x27;s generated head was shaking twice as hard as a real signer.

13
00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.740
The next project was just for art, 75,000 particles pulled around by hand landmarks.

14
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.440
Those same landmarks later became one of the data paths in Sign-Translator.

15
00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.940
All that curiosity led me to Sign-Translator.

16
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:14.080
Lines from two cameras meet at each joint, which gives its position in 3D.

17
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:20.500
Because bones don&#x27;t change length, they took the focal length from over 40% wrong to within 1%.

18
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:25.960
The motion is then compressed into codes, like a vocabulary, and a transformer writes them from English.

19
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.200
Then nearly everything failed.

20
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.060
The whole sentence generators read no better than standing still.

21
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:37.860
One of them passed 95 out of 95 motion checks and still couldn&#x27;t carry a sentence.

22
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:42.800
So now the system generates each word separately and stitches the clips together.

23
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.660
Words it doesn&#x27;t know simply vanish.

24
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.560
To score it, a model reads the motion back into English.

25
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.940
Blank motion scores 2.05, the generated motion 2.4, and real signing about 4.

26
00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:56.940
It&#x27;s a narrow gain, but it&#x27;s a start.

27
00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:58.140
Thanks for watching.
